\f0\fs24 \cf2 In the grand salon of the Bagatti Valsecchi Museum, the raised red velvet upholstery on a gold background immediately draws the eye. The main motif is an elegant vase filled with thistle and tulip flowers, with a long, double-pointed shape. This checkerboard pattern, featuring alternating oval forms, evokes the 16th century and harmonizes perfectly with the room\'92s ambiance. At the center of the ovals are heraldic symbols such as the eagle and lilies of the Bagatti Valsecchi family, as well as the abbreviation of their surname, "BA VA." The textile was crafted by Ambrogio Osnago, a renowned 19th-century Milanese weaver, who masterfully blended refinement and craftsmanship into an unparalleled work.\
}